5.Which of the following helps with accessibility in CSS?
A)Using very small fonts
B)Ensuring high color contrast via CSS //answers
C)Removing all backgrounds
D)Using only one font


✅ Correct Answer: B) Ensuring high color contrast via CSS

Simple English:

High color contrast makes text easy to read for everyone, including people with vision problems.

Example:
body {
  color: black;
  background: white;
}

👉 Black text on a white background is easy to read.

Easy Remember:

High Contrast = Better Readability = Better Accessibility

Options:
❌ A) Using very small fonts
✅ B) Ensuring high color contrast via CSS
❌ C) Removing all backgrounds
❌ D) Using only one font

✅ Final Answer: B) Ensuring high color contrast via CSS

6.What is a major advantage of using External CSS?
A)It makes the HTML file larger
B)It allows you to change the look of an entire website by changing one file //answer
C)It only works in Chrome
D)It requires no internet


✅ Correct Answer: B) It allows you to change the look of an entire website by changing one file

Simple English:

External CSS means your CSS is in a separate .css file.

If many HTML pages use the same CSS file:

✅ Change one CSS file
✅ All pages update automatically
Example:
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">

If you change style.css, every page using it gets the new design.

Easy Remember:

One CSS file → Update whole website

Options:
❌ A) It makes the HTML file larger
✅ B) It allows you to change the look of an entire website by changing one file
❌ C) It only works in Chrome
❌ D) It requires no internet

✅ Final Answer: B) It allows you to change the look of an entire website by changing one file



7.Which of the following is a limitation of CSS?

A)tcannot handle colors
B)It cannot be used with HTML
C)Browsers may interpret CSS differently (Cross-browser inconsistency) //answer
D)It only works on Windows


✅ Correct Answer: C) Browsers may interpret CSS differently (Cross-browser inconsistency)

Simple English:

A limitation of CSS is that different browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge) may display the same CSS slightly differently.

Example:

A CSS effect may:

✅ Work perfectly in Chrome
⚠️ Look slightly different in Firefox or Safari

This is called cross-browser inconsistency.

Easy Remember:

Different Browser → Different Result

Options:
❌ A) It cannot handle colors
❌ B) It cannot be used with HTML
✅ C) Browsers may interpret CSS differently (Cross-browser inconsistency)
❌ D) It only works on Windows

✅ Final Answer: C) Browsers may interpret CSS differently (Cross-browser inconsistency)





